<article class="post">
<header class="post-header wrap-narrow">
  <span class="eyebrow">Benchmarks · Performance</span>
  <h1><%= post["title"] %></h1>
  <p class="post-meta"><time datetime="<%= post["date"] %>"><%= post["display_date"] %></time> · <%= post["read_minutes"] %> min read</p>
  <p class="post-lede">Same machine, same million documents, same queries, each database in a container of 4 cores. SoliDB answered a read by key in 15&nbsp;µs where PostgreSQL took 18, and at 32 clients served 734,000 of them a second, nearly twice PostgreSQL&rsquo;s 378,000. Of the 23 series we measured, SoliDB led 21. The other two went to PostgreSQL, by about 10%: a join at one client, and inserts fsynced before they are acknowledged at 32. Then a reader asked whether SoliDB is ACID, so we added durable transactions and <code>kill -9</code>, against PostgreSQL. The test caught SoliDB 2.9.0 losing money, which 2.9.1 fixes. PostgreSQL moved money faster: 1.4 to 3.5 times as fast with the transfer sent as five statements, and still about twice as fast when each database got it in one request. SoliDB led only among a few hot accounts. Here is how we measured, what we tuned, and where SoliDB still loses.</p>
  <p class="post-note">Every figure on this page was measured on November 9, 2026, with the harness in <a href="https://github.com/solisoft/solidb/tree/main/benchmarks/compare"><code>benchmarks/compare</code></a>: the same million documents and the same queries, sent to each database from Rust, through its official client (ArangoDB, which has none, through its HTTP API), one database at a time, each in a container limited to 4 cores and 16 GB: the other databases in one run, SoliDB again on the published 2.9.0 image once it was released. Each figure is the median of three 20-second runs. The code, the configuration of every server, the exact text of every query and the raw results are in that directory. The <a href="#transactions">transactions and crashes</a> section was added later the same day, with <code>bank.sh</code> from the same directory, on SoliDB 2.9.0, 2.9.1 and 2.9.2 and PostgreSQL 18.6.</p>
</header>

<div class="post-body wrap-narrow">
