<section id="rules"><h2>The machine and the rules</h2>
<p>One machine: an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (16 cores, 32 threads), 125&nbsp;GB of memory, two Samsung PM9A3 NVMe drives in RAID&nbsp;1, Rocky Linux 10.2 (kernel 6.12), rootful podman 5.8.2. Its busiest other process, an idle SoliDB instance, averages 3% of one core. The three runs of every read series landed within 10% of each other, SoliDB&rsquo;s within 2%.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>One database at a time.</strong> Each runs alone in its container, on 4 cores and their SMT siblings (<code>--cpuset-cpus 0-3,16-19</code>) with <code>--memory 16g</code>, its data on the NVMe array. The data directory is deleted before the next one starts.</li>
<li><strong>The client never competes for the database&rsquo;s cores.</strong> The harness runs on 8 other cores, on the other half of the chip (<code>taskset -c 8-15,24-31</code>). It records its own CPU use, and flags any series where it came near its limit; none did.</li>
<li><strong>Warm cache.</strong> A million orders fit in 16&nbsp;GB on every engine. This measures what a database does with data in memory, not a disk.</li>
</ul>
<div class="post-table"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Database</th><th>Version</th><th>Client</th><th>Settings beyond the defaults</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><pre>SoliDB</pre></td><td><pre>2.9.0, solistore engine</pre></td><td><pre>solidb-client, MessagePack driver</pre></td><td><pre>SOLIDB_ENGINE=solistore</pre></td></tr>
    <tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL</pre></td><td><pre>18.6</pre></td><td><pre>tokio-postgres, prepared statements</pre></td><td><pre>shared_buffers=4GB, effective_cache_size=12GB, max_connections=200</pre></td></tr>
    <tr><td><pre>MongoDB</pre></td><td><pre>9.0.4</pre></td><td><pre>mongodb crate 3.10</pre></td><td><pre>none</pre></td></tr>
    <tr><td><pre>ArangoDB</pre></td><td><pre>3.12.4-3 Community</pre></td><td><pre>HTTP API through reqwest</pre></td><td><pre>told the container&rsquo;s memory and cores</pre></td></tr>
    <tr><td><pre>SurrealDB</pre></td><td><pre>4.0.0, SurrealKV on disk</pre></td><td><pre>surrealdb crate 4.0, WebSocket</pre></td><td><pre>none</pre></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Every image is pinned by digest in <code>run.sh</code>, and each result records the exact <code>podman run</code> command. A few choices need saying out loud:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>SoliDB runs on solistore, which is not its default engine.</strong> A new data directory still gets RocksDB unless <code>SOLIDB_ENGINE=solistore</code> is set; the <a href="/blog/solistore-a-storage-engine-of-our-own">solistore post</a> explains why it exists. Every figure for SoliDB here is solistore&rsquo;s.</li>
<li><strong>PostgreSQL appears twice.</strong> <em>jsonb</em> stores each order as one <code>jsonb</code> column, the same document model as the others. <em>typed</em> gives the scalar fields real columns, as a PostgreSQL user would design it. Both have the same three indexes as everyone else.</li>
<li><strong>ArangoDB has no maintained Rust driver.</strong> Its official drivers for other languages speak the same HTTP API that the harness calls, with a keep-alive connection per client.</li>
<li><strong>Durability is matched, not left to defaults.</strong> No engine fsyncs every commit in the main runs: PostgreSQL with <code>synchronous_commit=off</code>, MongoDB with its default <code>w:1</code>, ArangoDB without <code>waitForSync</code>, SurrealDB with <code>sync=never</code>, SoliDB with its default. The writes are then repeated with an fsync before each acknowledgement, in <a href="#writes">a section of their own</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The data</strong> is generated from a fixed seed, so every engine gets the same documents: 1,000,000 orders of about 500 bytes of JSON (a customer, a status, an amount, a date over two years, a country, three tags, three line items, an address) and 100,000 customers. Indexes on <code>customer_id</code>, <code>created_at</code> and <code>status</code> exist before the load, as they would in an application.</p>
<p><strong>The queries</strong> are each engine&rsquo;s idiom with bound parameters, drawn at random for every request so no cache can answer twice; SoliDB&rsquo;s query cache is off. Here is the join, as each engine received it:</p>
<div class="code-card post-code"><div class="cc-bar"><span class="dotk"></span> join, as sent</div><pre><span class="tok-com"># SoliDB</span>
FOR o IN orders FILTER o.created_at &gt;= @lo AND o.created_at &lt; @hi SORT o.created_at DESC LIMIT 20 LET c = DOCUMENT(CONCAT("customers/", o.customer_id)) RETURN {order: o._key, amount: o.amount, name: c.name, tier: c.tier}

<span class="tok-com"># PostgreSQL jsonb</span>
SELECT o.key, (o.doc-&gt;&gt;'amount')::float8 AS amount, c.doc-&gt;&gt;'name' AS name, c.doc-&gt;&gt;'tier' AS tier FROM (SELECT key, doc FROM orders WHERE doc-&gt;&gt;'created_at' &gt;= $1 AND doc-&gt;&gt;'created_at' &lt; $2 ORDER BY doc-&gt;&gt;'created_at' DESC LIMIT 20) o JOIN customers c ON c.key = o.doc-&gt;&gt;'customer_id'

<span class="tok-com"># PostgreSQL typed</span>
SELECT o.key, o.amount, c.name, c.tier FROM (SELECT key, amount, customer_id FROM orders WHERE created_at &gt;= $1 AND created_at &lt; $2 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20) o JOIN customers c ON c.key = o.customer_id

<span class="tok-com"># MongoDB</span>
orders.aggregate([{ "$match": { "created_at": { "$gte": "lo", "$lt": "hi" } } }, { "$sort": { "created_at": -1 } }, { "$limit": 20 }, { "$lookup": { "from": "customers", "localField": "customer_id", "foreignField": "_id", "as": "c" } }, { "$unwind": "$c" }, { "$project": { "_id": 1, "amount": 1, "name": "$c.name", "tier": "$c.tier" } }])

<span class="tok-com"># ArangoDB</span>
FOR o IN orders FILTER o.created_at &gt;= @lo AND o.created_at &lt; @hi SORT o.created_at DESC LIMIT 20 LET c = DOCUMENT(CONCAT("customers/", o.customer_id)) RETURN {order: o._key, amount: o.amount, name: c.name, tier: c.tier}

<span class="tok-com"># SurrealDB</span>
SELECT id, amount, customer.name AS name, customer.tier AS tier FROM orders WHERE created_at &gt;= $lo AND created_at &lt; $hi ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20</pre></div>
<p><strong>Every answer is checked, timed ones included.</strong> Before anything is timed, each read is compared 50 times with what the generator says it must return, and a wrong count stops the run, so a misconfigured index cannot win. While the clock runs, every answer is checked again: the document a key names, the number of rows a filter or a window must return, the customers a join must name, the count and the sum of every status in the aggregation. Across the 353,363,474 timed requests measured here, none got a wrong answer or an error.</p>
<p><strong>The client decodes what it receives</strong>, the same way everywhere: rows are deserialized into the same Rust structs, through the serde support each client documents (<code>query_as</code> for SoliDB, <code>Json&lt;T&gt;</code> for PostgreSQL, a typed <code>Collection&lt;T&gt;</code> for MongoDB, a typed response body for ArangoDB, <code>take::&lt;Vec&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> for SurrealDB). At one client, decoding is a large share of a query&rsquo;s time, so leaving it out, or letting one engine decode into a generic JSON tree while another reads typed columns, would change the ranking.</p>
<p><strong>Timing.</strong> Each series runs at 1 and 32 concurrent clients: 5 seconds of warm-up, then three runs of 20 seconds, of which the page shows the median. One client measures latency; 32 measure throughput. All reads run before any write, so every engine reads the same million documents.</p>
</section>

<section id="tuned"><h2>We tuned SoliDB against this benchmark</h2>
<p>You should know this before reading any number below: the first run of this harness, on SoliDB 2.7.2, went badly, and the releases since were shaped by it. The other databases ran with the defaults above and nobody tuned them.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The published Docker image did not start.</strong> Every image from 2.5.0 to 2.7.2 exited at once (a glibc older than the binary&rsquo;s), listened on loopback only, and ignored <code>SIGTERM</code>. Fixed in 2.7.3, and CI now starts the image before publishing it.</li>
<li><strong>A one-day window took 2.2 seconds.</strong> <code>created_at &gt;= @lo AND created_at &lt; @hi</code> read every index entry on one side of one bound. Thirty-two of those at once got the server killed for memory. 2.7.3 reads the interval only, and walks it in index order for <code>SORT … LIMIT</code>: 0.11&nbsp;ms.</li>
<li><strong>Writes cost twice the CPU they needed</strong>, and concurrent bulk inserts into one collection ran one at a time behind 256 striped locks. 2.8.0 locks each key alone, and halved the cycles of a load.</li>
<li><strong>A full-scan <code>COLLECT</code> decoded every document.</strong> 2.8.0 reads the grouped fields from the stored bytes and splits the scan across cores.</li>
<li><strong>2.9.0</strong>, released the day of this run, stores driver writes without decoding them, reads several keys from solistore in one pass, splits scans across cores on solistore too, and caches parsed queries.</li>
<li><strong>SoliDB&rsquo;s Rust client was tuned as well.</strong> The harness builds <code>solidb-client</code> from this repository (<code>clients/rust-client</code>), which 2.9.0 changed to read responses into a kept buffer and decode them by hand. The other databases are driven through their published clients, unchanged.</li>
</ul>
<p>None of these is specific to this benchmark&rsquo;s data, and each has its own tests. But a harness you have tuned against flatters you, so take every figure below as SoliDB at its best on this workload. The 2.7.2 results are kept beside the new ones in the repository for comparison.</p>
</section>

<section id="load"><h2>Loading a million documents</h2>
<p>Each engine first receives 100,000 customers, then the first 100,000 orders from one connection, then the remaining 900,000 from eight, always in batches of 1,000 through the bulk form each one documents: <code>bulk_insert</code> on the SoliDB driver, one <code>INSERT … SELECT FROM unnest(…)</code> per batch for PostgreSQL, <code>insertMany</code> unordered for MongoDB, a batch <code>POST /_api/document</code> for ArangoDB, <code>INSERT INTO orders $docs</code> for SurrealDB. The three indexes are already in place, so every engine maintains them as it goes.</p><p>From eight connections SoliDB stored 471,251 orders a second, 1.56 times PostgreSQL jsonb (302,399), the next fastest. From one connection it stored 188,294, against MongoDB&rsquo;s 104,696. SurrealDB, the slowest, stored 54,486 from eight.</p>
