</section>

<section id="scan"><h2>Scanning everything</h2>
<p>A query no index helps: count the orders and sum their amounts by status, over all million of them. One client sends it again and again for 20 seconds.</p><p>SoliDB answers in 37&nbsp;ms, PostgreSQL typed in 70, PostgreSQL jsonb in 145, MongoDB in 154, SurrealDB in 218 and ArangoDB in 959. Read the second chart before the first: SoliDB splits the scan across all 8 hardware threads of its container and keeps 7.4 of them busy, PostgreSQL uses its default of two parallel workers (2.9), and the others scan on one thread. Per query, that makes SoliDB the fastest and not the cheapest; <a href="#loses">more on that below</a>.</p>
<figure class="post-figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 286" role="img" aria-labelledby="cmp-aggregate-1-t"><title id="cmp-aggregate-1-t">Count and sum by status over 1M orders, one client (median per query)</title><text x="20" y="30" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#9ba39c">Count and sum by status over 1M orders, one client (median per query)</text><line x1="170" y1="44" x2="170" y2="246" stroke="#29322f"/><text x="20" y="64" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">SoliDB</text><rect x="170" y="48" width="16" height="22" rx="3" fill="#f5a623"/><text x="194" y="64" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">37.3 ms</text><text x="20" y="98" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">PostgreSQL jsonb</text><rect x="170" y="82" width="61" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="239" y="98" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">145.3 ms</text><text x="20" y="132" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">PostgreSQL typed</text><rect x="170" y="116" width="29" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="207" y="132" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">69.9 ms</text><text x="20" y="166" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">MongoDB</text><rect x="170" y="150" width="64" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="242" y="166" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">153.8 ms</text><text x="20" y="200" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">ArangoDB</text><rect x="170" y="184" width="400" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="578" y="200" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">959.3 ms</text><text x="20" y="234" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">SurrealDB</text><rect x="170" y="218" width="91" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="269" y="234" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">218.0 ms</text><text x="20" y="274" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#6b746c">Bars share one scale starting at 0; lower is better. Full scan, no index.</text></svg></figure>
<figure class="post-figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 286" role="img" aria-labelledby="cmp-aggregate-cpu-t"><title id="cmp-aggregate-cpu-t">The same query: database CPU time per query (core-milliseconds)</title><text x="20" y="30" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#9ba39c">The same query: database CPU time per query (core-milliseconds)</text><line x1="170" y1="44" x2="170" y2="246" stroke="#29322f"/><text x="20" y="64" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">SoliDB</text><rect x="170" y="48" width="114" height="22" rx="3" fill="#f5a623"/><text x="292" y="64" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">277</text><text x="20" y="98" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">PostgreSQL jsonb</text><rect x="170" y="82" width="176" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="354" y="98" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">429</text><text x="20" y="132" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">PostgreSQL typed</text><rect x="170" y="116" width="84" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="262" y="132" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">205</text><text x="20" y="166" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">MongoDB</text><rect x="170" y="150" width="63" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="241" y="166" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">154</text><text x="20" y="200" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">ArangoDB</text><rect x="170" y="184" width="400" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="578" y="200" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">973</text><text x="20" y="234" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">SurrealDB</text><rect x="170" y="218" width="102" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="280" y="234" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">248</text><text x="20" y="274" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#6b746c">Bars share one scale starting at 0; lower is better. Latency times cores used.</text></svg></figure>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>per query (p50)</th><th>p99</th><th>DB cores used</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>SoliDB</pre></td><td><pre><strong>37.3 ms</strong></pre></td><td><pre>39.0 ms</pre></td><td><pre>7.4</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL jsonb</pre></td><td><pre>145.3 ms</pre></td><td><pre>146.4 ms</pre></td><td><pre>3.0</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL typed</pre></td><td><pre>69.9 ms</pre></td><td><pre>70.4 ms</pre></td><td><pre>2.9</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>MongoDB</pre></td><td><pre>153.8 ms</pre></td><td><pre>156.4 ms</pre></td><td><pre>1.0</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>ArangoDB</pre></td><td><pre>959.3 ms</pre></td><td><pre>969.2 ms</pre></td><td><pre>1.0</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SurrealDB</pre></td><td><pre>218.0 ms</pre></td><td><pre>228.5 ms</pre></td><td><pre>1.1</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="code-card post-code"><div class="cc-bar"><span class="dotk"></span> aggregate, as sent</div><pre><span class="tok-com"># SoliDB</span>
FOR o IN orders COLLECT status = o.status AGGREGATE n = COUNT(), total = SUM(o.amount) RETURN {status, n, total}

<span class="tok-com"># PostgreSQL jsonb</span>
SELECT doc-&gt;&gt;'status' AS status, count(*) AS n, sum((doc-&gt;&gt;'amount')::float8) AS total FROM orders GROUP BY 1

<span class="tok-com"># PostgreSQL typed</span>
SELECT status, count(*) AS n, sum(amount) AS total FROM orders GROUP BY status

<span class="tok-com"># MongoDB</span>
orders.aggregate([{ "$group": { "_id": "$status", "n": { "$sum": 1 }, "total": { "$sum": "$amount" } } }])

<span class="tok-com"># ArangoDB</span>
FOR o IN orders COLLECT status = o.status AGGREGATE n = COUNT(1), total = SUM(o.amount) RETURN {status, n, total}

<span class="tok-com"># SurrealDB</span>
SELECT status, count() AS n, math::sum(amount) AS total FROM orders GROUP BY status</pre></div>
<p>Splitting a scan across threads is only worth it if the answer does not change. The harness checks every one of these answers, count and sum for each status, against the generator. And SoliDB&rsquo;s own tests (<a href="https://github.com/solisoft/solidb/blob/main/tests/sdbql_streaming_collect_tests.rs"><code>tests/sdbql_streaming_collect_tests.rs</code></a>, run in CI on both of its engines) compare a scan split across 2 to 16 threads with the same scan on one: the same groups, sums exact whatever the split, every document read once, and every range read at one snapshot, so that a write made after the scan began is seen by none of them.</p>
</section>

<section id="writes"><h2>Writing one document</h2>
<p>One document per request: <em>insert</em> adds a new order, <em>update</em> sets the <code>status</code> of an existing one, by key. These are the runs without an fsync on every commit. A <code>!</code> marks a series whose three runs differ by more than 10%: an insert run adds millions of orders, so the third run writes into a larger collection than the first.</p><p>At one client SoliDB inserts in 24&nbsp;µs, PostgreSQL in 30, ArangoDB in 40, MongoDB in 41 and SurrealDB in 491; it updates in 25&nbsp;µs, against MongoDB&rsquo;s 33 and PostgreSQL typed&rsquo;s 34. At 32 clients it inserts 158,777 orders a second, against 141,703 for PostgreSQL typed, and updates 183,742, against 115,389 for MongoDB. Since 2.9.0 the SoliDB driver stores an insert or a merge update straight from the client&rsquo;s MessagePack, decoding only the fields an index reads.</p>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>c=1 ops/s</th><th>c=1 p50</th><th>c=1 p99</th><th>c=32 ops/s</th><th>c=32 p50</th><th>c=32 p99</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>SoliDB</pre></td><td><pre><strong>40,785</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>24 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>30 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>158,777 !</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>135 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>864 µs</strong></pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL jsonb</pre></td><td><pre>32,321</pre></td><td><pre>30 µs</pre></td><td><pre>40 µs</pre></td><td><pre>135,277 !</pre></td><td><pre>165 µs</pre></td><td><pre>1.5 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL typed</pre></td><td><pre>32,472</pre></td><td><pre>30 µs</pre></td><td><pre>40 µs</pre></td><td><pre>141,703 !</pre></td><td><pre>158 µs</pre></td><td><pre>1.3 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>MongoDB</pre></td><td><pre>23,099</pre></td><td><pre>41 µs</pre></td><td><pre>57 µs</pre></td><td><pre>94,169 !</pre></td><td><pre>161 µs</pre></td><td><pre>2.5 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>ArangoDB</pre></td><td><pre>24,114</pre></td><td><pre>40 µs</pre></td><td><pre>49 µs</pre></td><td><pre>64,753 !</pre></td><td><pre>454 µs</pre></td><td><pre>1.3 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SurrealDB</pre></td><td><pre>2,012</pre></td><td><pre>491 µs</pre></td><td><pre>543 µs</pre></td><td><pre>11,292</pre></td><td><pre>2.7 ms</pre></td><td><pre>5.2 ms</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>c=1 ops/s</th><th>c=1 p50</th><th>c=1 p99</th><th>c=32 ops/s</th><th>c=32 p50</th><th>c=32 p99</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>SoliDB</pre></td><td><pre><strong>39,366</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>25 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>33 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>183,742</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>153 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>566 µs</strong></pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL jsonb</pre></td><td><pre>28,360</pre></td><td><pre>36 µs</pre></td><td><pre>61 µs</pre></td><td><pre>109,955</pre></td><td><pre>238 µs</pre></td><td><pre>668 µs</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL typed</pre></td><td><pre>25,386</pre></td><td><pre>34 µs</pre></td><td><pre>139 µs</pre></td><td><pre>105,140</pre></td><td><pre>214 µs</pre></td><td><pre>973 µs</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>MongoDB</pre></td><td><pre>29,556</pre></td><td><pre>33 µs</pre></td><td><pre>51 µs</pre></td><td><pre>115,389</pre></td><td><pre>206 µs</pre></td><td><pre>1.2 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>ArangoDB</pre></td><td><pre>19,292</pre></td><td><pre>50 µs</pre></td><td><pre>73 µs</pre></td><td><pre>44,489</pre></td><td><pre>661 µs</pre></td><td><pre>1.7 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SurrealDB</pre></td><td><pre>1,939</pre></td><td><pre>518 µs</pre></td><td><pre>569 µs</pre></td><td><pre>11,012</pre></td><td><pre>2.9 ms</pre></td><td><pre>6.0 ms</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>With an fsync before every acknowledgement</h3><p>The same writes, and the same load, with every engine set to make a write durable before answering: PostgreSQL with its default <code>synchronous_commit=on</code>, MongoDB with write concern <code>{w: 1, j: true}</code>, ArangoDB with <code>waitForSync</code> on both collections, SurrealDB with its default <code>sync=every</code>, and SoliDB with <code>SOLIDB_DURABILITY=full</code>, where concurrent commits share one fsync.</p><p>At one client SoliDB inserts in 39&nbsp;µs and PostgreSQL in 49. At 32 clients PostgreSQL inserts 89,587 orders a second and SoliDB 81,298: the second series SoliDB loses. PostgreSQL&rsquo;s three runs spread by 33% and its 99th percentile is 1.97&nbsp;ms against SoliDB&rsquo;s 634&nbsp;µs, but its median run is the faster one. For updates at 32 clients SoliDB leads, 81,902 a second against MongoDB&rsquo;s 75,085 and PostgreSQL&rsquo;s 49,732.</p>
<figure class="post-figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 252" role="img" aria-labelledby="cmp-insert-durable-32-t"><title id="cmp-insert-durable-32-t">Inserts fsynced before they are acknowledged, 32 clients (per second)</title><text x="20" y="30" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#9ba39c">Inserts fsynced before they are acknowledged, 32 clients (per second)</text><line x1="170" y1="44" x2="170" y2="212" stroke="#29322f"/><text x="20" y="64" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">SoliDB</text><rect x="170" y="48" width="363" height="22" rx="3" fill="#f5a623"/><text x="541" y="64" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">81,298</text><text x="20" y="98" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">PostgreSQL</text><rect x="170" y="82" width="400" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="578" y="98" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">89,587</text><text x="20" y="132" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">MongoDB</text><rect x="170" y="116" width="270" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="448" y="132" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">60,579</text><text x="20" y="166" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">ArangoDB</text><rect x="170" y="150" width="82" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="260" y="166" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">18,371</text><text x="20" y="200" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">SurrealDB</text><rect x="170" y="184" width="46" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="224" y="200" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">10,272</text><text x="20" y="240" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#6b746c">Bars share one scale starting at 0; higher is better. One document per request.</text></svg></figure>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>c=1 ops/s</th><th>c=1 p50</th><th>c=1 p99</th><th>c=32 ops/s</th><th>c=32 p50</th><th>c=32 p99</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>SoliDB</pre></td><td><pre><strong>23,343</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>39 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>52 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre>81,298</pre></td><td><pre>303 µs</pre></td><td><pre><strong>634 µs</strong></pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL</pre></td><td><pre>19,069</pre></td><td><pre>49 µs</pre></td><td><pre>75 µs</pre></td><td><pre><strong>89,587 !</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>278 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre>2.0 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>MongoDB</pre></td><td><pre>14,641</pre></td><td><pre>64 µs</pre></td><td><pre>98 µs</pre></td><td><pre>60,579 !</pre></td><td><pre>371 µs</pre></td><td><pre>3.4 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>ArangoDB</pre></td><td><pre>10,836</pre></td><td><pre>83 µs</pre></td><td><pre>120 µs</pre></td><td><pre>18,371 !</pre></td><td><pre>1.3 ms</pre></td><td><pre>5.9 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SurrealDB</pre></td><td><pre>1,780</pre></td><td><pre>545 µs</pre></td><td><pre>594 µs</pre></td><td><pre>10,272</pre></td><td><pre>2.9 ms</pre></td><td><pre>5.8 ms</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>c=1 ops/s</th><th>c=1 p50</th><th>c=1 p99</th><th>c=32 ops/s</th><th>c=32 p50</th><th>c=32 p99</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>SoliDB</pre></td><td><pre><strong>22,514</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>40 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>56 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>81,902</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>343 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>671 µs</strong></pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL</pre></td><td><pre>14,954</pre></td><td><pre>58 µs</pre></td><td><pre>110 µs</pre></td><td><pre>49,732</pre></td><td><pre>403 µs</pre></td><td><pre>5.5 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>MongoDB</pre></td><td><pre>18,210</pre></td><td><pre>53 µs</pre></td><td><pre>92 µs</pre></td><td><pre>75,085</pre></td><td><pre>355 µs</pre></td><td><pre>994 µs</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>ArangoDB</pre></td><td><pre>9,199 !</pre></td><td><pre>93 µs</pre></td><td><pre>132 µs</pre></td><td><pre>19,414</pre></td><td><pre>1.3 ms</pre></td><td><pre>5.1 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SurrealDB</pre></td><td><pre>1,714 !</pre></td><td><pre>569 µs</pre></td><td><pre>631 µs</pre></td><td><pre>10,279</pre></td><td><pre>3.1 ms</pre></td><td><pre>6.6 ms</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>customers, 8 loaders</th><th>orders, 1 loader</th><th>orders, 8 loaders</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>SoliDB</pre></td><td><pre><strong>1,703,652</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>175,108</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>386,531</strong></pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL</pre></td><td><pre>842,831</pre></td><td><pre>89,688</pre></td><td><pre>174,940</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>MongoDB</pre></td><td><pre>736,803</pre></td><td><pre>104,626</pre></td><td><pre>228,959</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>ArangoDB</pre></td><td><pre>1,114,840</pre></td><td><pre>72,573</pre></td><td><pre>221,408</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SurrealDB</pre></td><td><pre>722,199</pre></td><td><pre>29,925</pre></td><td><pre>50,428</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</section>

<section id="transactions"><h2>Transactions, and a kill -9</h2>
<p>A reader answered the first version of this post: speed doesn&rsquo;t matter, correctness does; is SoliDB ACID compliant? It is a fair question, and no series above answers it. So we added one that does, against PostgreSQL, and published it beside the rest (<code>bank.sh</code> and <code>src/bank/</code>, same machine, same containers).</p>
<p><strong>The workload moves money.</strong> Each transfer is one transaction: take 1 to 100 from an account, only if it holds that much; give it to another; write a ledger row. Every account starts at 1,000, and every commit is fsynced before it is acknowledged: PostgreSQL with <code>synchronous_commit=on</code>, SoliDB with <code>SOLIDB_DURABILITY=full</code>. A transfer refused by the concurrency control (a lock held, a serialization failure, a stale write) is retried, with the same backoff on both. Each database does it the way its documentation would:</p>
<div class="code-card post-code"><div class="cc-bar"><span class="dotk"></span> the debit, as sent</div><pre><span class="tok-com"># SoliDB: five requests (begin, debit, credit, ledger, commit), over HTTP or, from 2.9.2, its binary driver</span>
FOR a IN accounts FILTER a._key == @k FILTER a.balance &gt;= @x UPDATE a WITH {balance: a.balance - @x} IN accounts

<span class="tok-com"># PostgreSQL, READ COMMITTED: BEGIN, debit and credit in account order (no deadlocks), INSERT, COMMIT</span>
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - $2 WHERE id = $1 AND balance &gt;= $2

<span class="tok-com"># PostgreSQL, SERIALIZABLE: read both balances, decide in the client, write both</span>
SELECT id, balance FROM accounts WHERE id = ANY($1)</pre></div>
<p><strong>What is checked.</strong> After every run, the balances must add up to what they started with, none may be negative, each must equal what the acknowledged transfers moved and what the stored ledger says, and every acknowledged transfer must have its ledger row. While the transfers run, another client sums every balance in one statement every 10&nbsp;ms and must always see the full total. A control configuration, deliberately unsafe (PostgreSQL reading a balance and writing it back at READ COMMITTED), makes sure the checks catch a lost update. They did, in every run.</p>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>Runs</th><th>Checks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, READ COMMITTED, conditional UPDATE</pre></td><td><pre>9</pre></td><td><pre>all passed</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, SERIALIZABLE, read then write</pre></td><td><pre>9</pre></td><td><pre>all passed</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, read then write at READ COMMITTED (control)</pre></td><td><pre>3</pre></td><td><pre>money invented in all 3</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.0, SDBQL</pre></td><td><pre>9</pre></td><td><pre><strong>money lost or invented in 6</strong></pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.0, documents</pre></td><td><pre>9</pre></td><td><pre>all passed</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.1, SDBQL</pre></td><td><pre>9</pre></td><td><pre>all passed</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.1, documents</pre></td><td><pre>9</pre></td><td><pre>all passed</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.2, SDBQL over HTTP</pre></td><td><pre>9</pre></td><td><pre>all passed</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.2, SDBQL over the driver</pre></td><td><pre>9</pre></td><td><pre>all passed</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The test found a bug in SoliDB.</strong> 2.9.0, the version measured in the rest of this post, lost and invented money in every run of the SDBQL version, even among 10,000 accounts with little contention. An <code>UPDATE</code> computed its new value from the document as the query had read it, then overwrote any write that landed in between, inside a transaction or not, at every isolation level. Our fault-injection harness had caught it a few hours earlier. 2.9.1, released the same day, carries the revision the query read with every such write: it re-evaluates the row under the key&rsquo;s lock, or refuses the transaction with a conflict. It passed every check in all 18 of its runs, at the same throughput, and so did 2.9.2 in its 18. Writing through the document API (read, then write, inside the transaction) was correct on both versions.</p>
<p><strong>Over HTTP, PostgreSQL was much faster.</strong> SoliDB 2.9.0 and 2.9.1 could only send this transfer through their HTTP API, and PostgreSQL committed 2.6 times as many at one client, 3.5 times as many at 32, and 1.6 times as many among 10 hot accounts. The cost was not where you would guess. A profile put under 1% of SoliDB&rsquo;s CPU in locks and transaction bookkeeping, and about half in the HTTP stack, its authorization middleware and the kernel. SoliDB&rsquo;s binary driver refused a query that writes inside a transaction. 2.9.2, released the same evening, accepts it, and the same statements commit about twice as often:</p>
<figure class="post-figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 218" role="img" aria-labelledby="cmp-bank-32-t"><title id="cmp-bank-32-t">Durable transfers, 10,000 accounts, 32 clients (per second)</title><text x="20" y="30" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#9ba39c">Durable transfers, 10,000 accounts, 32 clients (per second)</text><line x1="170" y1="44" x2="170" y2="178" stroke="#29322f"/><text x="20" y="64" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">SoliDB driver</text><rect x="170" y="48" width="229" height="22" rx="3" fill="#f5a623"/><text x="407" y="64" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">38,518</text><text x="20" y="98" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">SoliDB HTTP</text><rect x="170" y="82" width="116" height="22" rx="3" fill="#f5a623"/><text x="294" y="98" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">19,561</text><text x="20" y="132" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">PostgreSQL RC</text><rect x="170" y="116" width="400" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="578" y="132" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">67,383</text><text x="20" y="166" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">PostgreSQL SER</text><rect x="170" y="150" width="274" height="22" rx="3" fill="#6b746c"/><text x="452" y="166" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#ece6d6">46,138</text><text x="20" y="206" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#6b746c">Bars share one scale starting at 0; higher is better. SoliDB 2.9.2. RC: READ COMMITTED. SER: SERIALIZABLE.</text></svg></figure>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>10,000 accounts, 1 client</th><th>p50</th><th>10,000 accounts, 32 clients</th><th>p99</th><th>10 accounts, 32 clients</th><th>p99</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, READ COMMITTED</pre></td><td><pre><strong>10,555</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>92 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>67,383</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>1.4 ms</strong></pre></td><td><pre>8,339</pre></td><td><pre><strong>44 ms</strong></pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, SERIALIZABLE</pre></td><td><pre>8,361</pre></td><td><pre>118 µs</pre></td><td><pre>46,138</pre></td><td><pre>1.6 ms</pre></td><td><pre>7,663</pre></td><td><pre>109 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.2, SDBQL over the driver</pre></td><td><pre>7,563</pre></td><td><pre>128 µs</pre></td><td><pre>38,518</pre></td><td><pre>1.4 ms</pre></td><td><pre><strong>9,369</strong></pre></td><td><pre>73 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.2, SDBQL over HTTP</pre></td><td><pre>4,061</pre></td><td><pre>238 µs</pre></td><td><pre>19,561</pre></td><td><pre>3.1 ms</pre></td><td><pre>5,189</pre></td><td><pre>103 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.1, documents over HTTP</pre></td><td><pre>3,458</pre></td><td><pre>285 µs</pre></td><td><pre>18,874</pre></td><td><pre>3.3 ms</pre></td><td><pre>2,865</pre></td><td><pre>203 ms</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Among 10 hot accounts, SoliDB over its driver now commits 12% more than PostgreSQL. Without contention PostgreSQL is still ahead, 1.4 times at one client and 1.75 times at 32. Under contention, SoliDB&rsquo;s transaction locks refuse at once instead of waiting: over the driver that is 1.4 rolled-back attempts for every transfer that commits, and a 99th percentile of 73&nbsp;ms against PostgreSQL&rsquo;s 44.</p>
<p><strong>Then one request each.</strong> Five round trips per transfer are a client&rsquo;s choice, not the database&rsquo;s. The next SoliDB version adds <code>run_transaction</code>, which sends the begin, the statements and the commit together; a statement can be marked to roll everything back if it matches nothing, which is how the debit checks the balance. PostgreSQL has had the same for decades: the three statements in a PL/pgSQL function, called in one round trip. Measured on a build of SoliDB&rsquo;s <code>main</code>, released the next day as 2.9.3:</p>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>One request per transfer</th><th>10,000 accounts, 1 client</th><th>p50</th><th>10,000 accounts, 32 clients</th><th>p99</th><th>10 accounts, 32 clients</th><th>DB CPU per transfer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, PL/pgSQL function</pre></td><td><pre><strong>21,674</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>44 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>113,920</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>556 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre>10,406</pre></td><td><pre><strong>24–54 µs</strong></pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB, run_transaction (main)</pre></td><td><pre>9,919</pre></td><td><pre>110 µs</pre></td><td><pre>61,828</pre></td><td><pre>1.1 ms</pre></td><td><pre><strong>15,648</strong></pre></td><td><pre>103–113 µs</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>One request each, PostgreSQL commits 2.2 times as many transfers at one client and 1.8 times as many at 32; SoliDB still leads among hot accounts, by 1.5 times, though PostgreSQL&rsquo;s tail there is shorter (34&nbsp;ms against 44). Compared with PostgreSQL&rsquo;s five-statement version, SoliDB&rsquo;s single request would look level, which is why we measured both. With the round trips gone, what is left is the cost of a transaction itself: SoliDB spends two to four times PostgreSQL&rsquo;s CPU on one, and at one client its commit is 70 of the 110&nbsp;µs. A profile puts much of it in solistore&rsquo;s group commit, where commits spin while another one fsyncs. That is the next thing we are changing. (Done in solistore 0.3.2, shipped with SoliDB 2.9.3: <a href="/blog/durability-and-transactions#speed">the follow-up</a> has the numbers. And as of 2.9.4, two days after this post: 17,728 transfers a second at one client and 99,963 at 32 in one request, so PostgreSQL&rsquo;s lead is 1.22 and 1.14 times; over five round trips SoliDB is ahead at 32 clients, 71,889 against 67,383; among hot accounts it commits 3.2 times as many, and its locks now wait. <a href="/blog/durability-and-transactions#v294">The figures</a>.)</p>
<p><strong>Then we killed them.</strong> Thirty-two clients transfer among 10,000 accounts. After a random 1 to 4 seconds the container gets <code>SIGKILL</code>, is started again on the same data and checked, twenty times for each configuration.</p>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>transfers acknowledged</th><th>in flight at a kill (landed)</th><th>acknowledged, then lost</th><th>half applied</th><th>ready again</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, synchronous_commit=on</pre></td><td><pre>2,861,750</pre></td><td><pre>258 (129)</pre></td><td><pre>0</pre></td><td><pre>0</pre></td><td><pre>408 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, synchronous_commit=off</pre></td><td><pre>3,657,389</pre></td><td><pre>110 (0)</pre></td><td><pre>25,517</pre></td><td><pre>0</pre></td><td><pre>458 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.1, durability full</pre></td><td><pre>786,337</pre></td><td><pre>277 (137)</pre></td><td><pre>0</pre></td><td><pre>0</pre></td><td><pre>271 ms</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.1, durability eventual (commit still fsynced)</pre></td><td><pre>787,233</pre></td><td><pre>298 (104)</pre></td><td><pre>0</pre></td><td><pre>0</pre></td><td><pre>272 ms</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Neither PostgreSQL with <code>synchronous_commit=on</code> nor SoliDB in either durability mode lost an acknowledged transfer or left one half applied. A transfer in flight at the kill landed or did not, and both happened. PostgreSQL with <code>synchronous_commit=off</code> lost the last commits before each kill, as its documentation says it may, but never part of one. <strong>Corrected on November 10:</strong> we first wrote here that SoliDB&rsquo;s <code>eventual</code> mode lost nothing because it hands every commit to the operating system and only delays the fsync. That line measured the same thing as the one above it: these transactions begin at <code>read_committed</code>, whose commit SoliDB fsyncs in either mode, and <code>SOLIDB_DURABILITY</code> decides only for writes outside a transaction. A commit acknowledged before its fsync (<code>read_uncommitted</code>) was killed twenty times the next day, on 2.9.3, and lost nothing; <a href="/blog/durability-and-transactions">Durability and transactions</a> has that test and the rest.</p>
<p><strong>So, is SoliDB ACID?</strong> On these tests, from 2.9.1: a transaction applies whole or not at all, a crash loses nothing acknowledged, and concurrent transfers conserve money. 2.9.0 did not, on a statement every application writes, and it took this question to find out. Some of it is still untested or not there yet. A power loss below the filesystem has not been simulated. Jepsen&rsquo;s Elle has not yet searched the isolation histories for cycles, which is how write skew would show. And the isolation level a SoliDB transaction asks for only decides whether its commit is fsynced; the <a href="/docs/transactions">transactions page</a> now says exactly that.</p>
</section>

<section id="footprint"><h2>Memory and disk</h2>
<p>Measured right after the load, when every engine holds the same 1.1 million documents and has served no read. Later figures are not comparable, since each engine ran a different number of inserts. The data directory is <code>du</code> of the bind mount; process memory is the container cgroup&rsquo;s anonymous memory, and page cache its file-backed memory.</p><p>SoliDB&rsquo;s data takes 454&nbsp;MB, MongoDB&rsquo;s 546, and PostgreSQL jsonb&rsquo;s 1,920. Memory needs a warning: PostgreSQL keeps its <code>shared_buffers</code> in shared memory, which the kernel counts as page cache, so its 123&nbsp;MB of process memory leaves out most of what it holds. MongoDB and SurrealDB keep the loaded data in caches of their own; SoliDB&rsquo;s block cache fills as reads arrive, and none had yet. These columns say what each engine holds at that moment, not what it needs.</p>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>data directory</th><th>process memory</th><th>page cache</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><pre>SoliDB</pre></td><td><pre><strong>454 MB</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>108 MB</strong></pre></td><td><pre>454 MB</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL jsonb</pre></td><td><pre>1,920 MB</pre></td><td><pre>123 MB</pre></td><td><pre>2,864 MB</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL typed</pre></td><td><pre>1,589 MB</pre></td><td><pre>134 MB</pre></td><td><pre>2,352 MB</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>MongoDB</pre></td><td><pre>546 MB</pre></td><td><pre>1,137 MB</pre></td><td><pre>435 MB</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>ArangoDB</pre></td><td><pre>1,210 MB</pre></td><td><pre>531 MB</pre></td><td><pre>1,210 MB</pre></td></tr><tr><td><pre>SurrealDB</pre></td><td><pre>748 MB</pre></td><td><pre>1,160 MB</pre></td><td><pre>748 MB</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</section>

<section id="loses"><h2>Where SoliDB loses</h2>
<p>Two of the 23 series went to PostgreSQL, so did two of the three series of the transaction test, and one result that SoliDB wins needs a qualification.</p><ul><li><strong>Durable transactions</strong>: with the transfer sent in one request by each, PostgreSQL commits 2.2 times as many at one client and 1.8 times as many at 32; SoliDB leads only among 10 hot accounts. (2.9.4, two days later: 1.22 and 1.14 times, <a href="/blog/durability-and-transactions#v294">measured here</a>.) As five statements over SoliDB 2.9.2&rsquo;s driver the gap is 1.4 and 1.75 times, and over HTTP 2.6 to 3.5. What is left is the cost of SoliDB&rsquo;s commit, two to four times PostgreSQL&rsquo;s CPU per transaction (<a href="#transactions">above</a>).</li><li><strong>The join at one client</strong>: 80&nbsp;µs at the median against PostgreSQL typed&rsquo;s 71. Profiling puts the difference in its 40 point reads, 20 orders and 20 customers, from solistore&rsquo;s block cache, where each read still takes a lock. Removing it is the next piece of work on solistore.</li><li><strong>Fsynced inserts at 32 clients</strong>: 81,298 a second against PostgreSQL&rsquo;s 89,587, even though SoliDB&rsquo;s tail is a third of PostgreSQL&rsquo;s. Measured again on 2.9.3 the next morning, beside PostgreSQL: 86,890 against 88,307, inside PostgreSQL&rsquo;s spread of 76,449 to 102,017 across its three runs.</li><li><strong>The full scan costs more CPU than it looks.</strong> SoliDB finishes first because it uses every core it has: 277 core-milliseconds per query, against 154 for MongoDB and 248 for SurrealDB, which scan on one thread, and 205 for PostgreSQL typed on three. A server busy with other queries will not give one scan eight threads.</li></ul><p>Then there is what this run does not measure. Everything here fits in memory; a data set larger than RAM, a cold start, replication and multi-node setups are left out. SoliDB runs on solistore, which is not yet its default engine. The other databases ran as their documentation sets them up and nobody tuned them, while SoliDB was tuned against this very harness. And PostgreSQL and MongoDB carry decades of production use that no benchmark captures.</p>
</section>

<section id="run"><h2>Run it yourself</h2>
<p>The harness needs Linux and Docker or rootful podman. The CPU sets in <code>run.sh</code> assume 16 cores; set <code>DB_CPUS</code> and <code>CLIENT_CPUS</code> for your machine.</p>
<div class="code-card post-code"><div class="cc-bar"><span class="dotk"></span> terminal</div><pre>git clone https://github.com/solisoft/solidb && cd solidb/benchmarks/compare
cargo build --release
./run.sh all                                    <span class="tok-com"># about three hours</span>
DOCS=10000 CUSTOMERS=1000 WARM=1 SECS=2 RUNS=1 ./run.sh all   <span class="tok-com"># a quick pass</span>
python3 report.py ~/bench/results/&lt;date&gt;       <span class="tok-com"># Markdown tables</span>
python3 post_figures.py ~/bench/results/&lt;date&gt; <span class="tok-com"># this page's tables and charts</span>
cargo build --release --bin solidb-bank
./bank.sh all                                   <span class="tok-com"># transactions and kill -9, about 45 minutes</span>
python3 bank_report.py ~/bench/results/&lt;date&gt;/bank</pre></div>
<p>With podman, set <code>DOCKER="sudo podman"</code>. Each engine is an <code>Engine</code> implementation of about 200 lines in <code>src/engines/</code>. If you think one of them is used wrongly, or a setting is unfair to a database, open an issue or a pull request against that file: we will rerun and update this page.</p>
</section>
</div>
